From fb0c053da212d313ffb2d071293fd9343fda5a84 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fzdark build <build@localhost>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:02:51 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] msw: resolve the control to draw from the notification's HWND

wxWindowMSW::MSWOnDrawItem() picked the control to draw with FindItem(id),
which matches on the control ID and ignores the HWND: wxControl::MSWFindItem()
in src/msw/control.cpp compares id against GetId() and never looks at the hWnd
parameter it is handed. WM_DRAWITEM carries the ID in wParam and the
control's own window in the structure at lParam, so the lookup has everything
it needs to be unambiguous and throws the HWND away.

It is only safe while no two children share an ID. When they do, the first
child created answers WM_DRAWITEM on behalf of the other, and:

  - If the winner has no MSWOnDraw() override it inherits
    wxControl::MSWOnDraw(), which returns false. Returning false tells Windows
    the message went unhandled, and because an owner-drawn control is never
    painted by Windows there is no fallback: the control that actually asked to
    be drawn is left with its space reserved and nothing in it at all.

  - If the winner does override it, the wrong control is drawn into the
    requesting control's rectangle.

Dark mode makes this reachable. src/msw/checkbox.cpp and src/msw/radiobut.cpp
both call MSWMakeOwnerDrawn(wxMSWDarkMode::IsActive() || m_hasFgCol), so in dark
mode every wxCheckBox and wxRadioButton becomes owner-drawn and the whole code
path is live. In light mode Windows paints them directly and the collision
never comes into play, which is what makes this look like a dark-mode bug
rather than a lookup bug.

Sharing an ID is not an application error. wxID_HIGHEST, exported as nullID, is
a deliberate choice for controls whose ID is never used - no events, no command
handling - and wx's own code does it, so an application following that idiom
gets unpainted check boxes.

Prefer the HWND from the notification and keep the ID lookup as a fallback, for
controls with no window of their own. HandleCtlColor() already resolves
WM_CTLCOLOR* this way, for the same reason and with the same cast, so this is
the lookup wx uses for the message that also arrives with an HWND in hand.

Reported against wxWidgets 3.3.3 with FileZilla 3.71.1, where the FileZilla
side of the collision is src/interface/verifyhostkeydialog.cpp: the
"Unknown host key" dialog's "Always trust this host" check box shares nullID
with the wxStaticBitmap icon created above it, and comes out blank in dark
mode. src/interface/settings/optionspage_interface.cpp is the same story for
"Swap local and remote panes".
---
 src/msw/window.cpp | 39 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/msw/window.cpp b/src/msw/window.cpp
index 57cef26..1d3b423 100644
--- a/src/msw/window.cpp
+++ b/src/msw/window.cpp
@@ -4802,19 +4802,40 @@ wxWindowMSW::MSWOnDrawItem(int WXUNUSED_UNLESS_ODRAWN(id),
 
 #if wxUSE_CONTROLS && !defined(__WXUNIVERSAL__)
 
+    // Resolve the control to draw from the HWND carried in the notification,
+    // which names exactly the control that asked to be drawn.
+    //
+    // The ID alone is not enough, and relying on it was a real bug: FindItem()
+    // matches on the ID and ignores the HWND, so when two children share an ID the
+    // first one created answers WM_DRAWITEM on behalf of the other. Sharing an ID
+    // is not a mistake applications have to make: wxID_HIGHEST, exposed by wx as
+    // nullID, is a popular choice precisely because the ID is then never used for
+    // anything. Under dark mode wxCheckBox and wxRadioButton are made owner-drawn
+    // (src/msw/checkbox.cpp and src/msw/radiobut.cpp) and Windows never falls back
+    // to painting an owner-drawn control itself, so a control that lost the lookup
+    // was left with its space reserved and nothing at all in it.
+    DRAWITEMSTRUCT *drawStruct = (DRAWITEMSTRUCT *)itemStruct;
+    wxControl *item = NULL;
+    if ( drawStruct->hwndItem )
+        item = wxDynamicCast(FindItemByHWND(drawStruct->hwndItem, true), wxControl);
+
 #if wxUSE_OWNER_DRAWN
-    wxControl *item = wxDynamicCast(FindItem(id), wxControl);
+    // Still fall back to the ID for anything the HWND cannot resolve, so controls
+    // that are not backed by a window of their own keep working.
+    if ( !item )
+        item = wxDynamicCast(FindItem(id), wxControl);
 #else // !wxUSE_OWNER_DRAWN
     // we may still have owner-drawn buttons internally because we have to make
     // them owner-drawn to support colour change
-    wxControl *item =
-#                     if wxUSE_BUTTON
-                         wxDynamicCast(FindItem(id), wxButton)
-#                     else
-                         nullptr
-#                     endif
-                    ;
-#endif // USE_OWNER_DRAWN
+    if ( !item )
+        item =
+#             if wxUSE_BUTTON
+                wxDynamicCast(FindItem(id), wxButton)
+#             else
+                nullptr
+#             endif
+            ;
+#endif // wxUSE_OWNER_DRAWN
 
     if ( item )
     {
-- 
